<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">First Ibadi Imamate of Oman</h1><p class="st-meta">751 CE – 893 CE (approx.) · capital Nizwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Ibadi state in Oman, an elected imamate centred on Nizwa that set the pattern for Omani religious government for a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 751 the Omani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadis</a> elected al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-julanda-ibn-masud">Julanda ibn Mas'ud</a> as their first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, but he was killed the next year by an army of the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. After decades under local chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> was restored in 793 with the election of Muhammad ibn Affan, followed by Warith ibn Ka'b, and lasted a century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a> were chosen by the learned and leading men and could be deposed if they broke the law, an ideal of accountable leadership central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> became the capital and a centre of scholarship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> controlled the sea trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sohar">Sohar</a>, while the falaj irrigation channels supported date farming in the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-salt-ibn-malik">al-Salt ibn Malik</a> ruled for over thirty years.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-salt-ibn-malik">al-Salt ibn Malik</a> in 886 split Omani scholars into rival schools and led to civil war. In 893 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a>, Muhammad ibn Nur, invaded, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, though Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> reappeared repeatedly later.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 751 CE – 893 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:74.65%;width:2.83%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>751 CE – 893 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nizwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>al-Julanda ibn Mas'ud, Ghassan ibn Abd Allah, al-Salt ibn Malik</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Imamate of Oman (751-893), Julanda Imamate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=822&amp;focus=first-ibadi-imamate-of-oman" aria-label="First Ibadi Imamate of Oman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6217 1331 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where First Ibadi Imamate of Oman lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6537 1587L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6537 1587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6537 1587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nizwa. <a href="/atlas?year=822&amp;focus=first-ibadi-imamate-of-oman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

